#____________________________________________________________________________________________
#
# RECOVERY QUANTITIES vs TRUNCATION RADIUS   (generated by tabulate_recovery.py)
# One row per (grid node, band, truncation radius r): a model source measured
# through a shrinking aperture.  Every quantity below is a monotonic function of
# r, so a real source can be matched afterwards on peak/noise, footprint, or
# concentration -- without recomputing anything and without knowing R_conv.
#____________________________________________________________________________________________
#
# TABULATED QUANTITIES:
#
#  1 i          -        cSD (background-column) grid index
#  2 j          -        mass grid index
#  3 k          -        xi_max (Bonnor-Ebert truncation) grid index
#  4 band       -        photometric band (SD = 13.5" H2 column; else wavelength/um)
#  5 SD_emb     cm^-2    embedding (background) column density
#  6 r_as       arcsec   truncation / aperture radius
#  7 r_Rconv    -        r / R_conv: aperture as a fraction of the full extent
#                        (R_conv is NOT observable -- match real sources on peak_rim)
#  8 peak       MJy/sr   central profile intensity (raw, not background-subtracted)
#  9 I_rim      MJy/sr   azimuthally-averaged profile intensity at r: the effective
#                        detection threshold (the rim / local background level)
# 10 peak_rim   -        peak / I_rim: model counterpart of an observed peak-to-noise
#                        ratio -- the variable with a real-data counterpart
# 11 frac       -        recovered flux fraction, normalised to 1 at r = R_conv
# 12 fwhm_rec   arcsec   FWHM of the truncated, rim-subtracted profile (half-max
#                        interpolated, to match getsf AFWHM/BFWHM)
# 13 cfoot      -        2 r / fwhm_rec: footprint-to-size ratio (the model R_foot)
# 14 cpeakmean  -        peak / mean intensity inside r (both rim-subtracted): the
#                        source concentration
# 15 rel        -        reliable flag: 1 if frac >= 0.20, else 0
# 16 absorp     -        absorption flag: 1 if the central pixel is negative (cold
#                        core against a warmer background; typical at 70/100 um and
#                        for depression models)
#____________________________________________________________________________________________
#
# NOTES ON nan VALUES (all expected -- none are computation errors):
#   absorp=1 : not a positive declining source, so I_rim, peak_rim, frac, fwhm_rec,
#              cfoot and cpeakmean are all nan by construction.
#   peak_rim : nan (with absorp=0) means I_rim <= 0 -- the rim lies in the
#              background-noise region at large r, where peak-to-noise is undefined.
#   fwhm_rec,cfoot,cpeakmean : nan (with absorp=0) means the source is heavily
#              truncated or low-contrast (typically frac < ~0.15) and the
#              rim-subtracted profile never reaches half peak, so a footprint size
#              cannot be defined.  Such rows carry rel=0.
